1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:03,702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03,652 --> 00:00:07,194
Today is November 05, 2026.

3
00:00:07,194 --> 00:00:14,314
Overnight, all three major U.S. indices closed higher after Democrats swept both chambers of Congress.

4
00:00:14,314 --> 00:00:20,491
The Dow led the charge, surging roughly three percent on a powerful rotation into cyclicals.

5
00:00:20,491 --> 00:00:27,537
The S&P 500 gapped higher, filled last Friday's gap, and reclaimed its six-month moving average.

6
00:00:27,537 --> 00:00:33,652
It now sits back inside its 7,600 to 7,800 trading range.

7
00:00:33,652 --> 00:00:39,682
The Nasdaq gained about one and a half percent, but faded more than 300 points off the high.

8
00:00:39,682 --> 00:00:47,599
The Nasdaq 100 tagged 28,900, former support now turned hard resistance, and sellers stepped in.

9
00:00:47,599 --> 00:00:53,849
Futures first plunged about 100 points on the results, then bottomed after midnight and reversed.

10
00:00:53,849 --> 00:00:59,731
The VIX collapsed from 23 to around 16 as institutions dumped their election hedges.

11
00:00:59,731 --> 00:01:06,140
The 10-year Treasury yield fell about seven basis points, to roughly 4.89 percent.

12
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:12,133
A Democratic Congress blocks new fiscal expansion, easing the outlook for Treasury supply.

13
00:01:12,133 --> 00:01:15,858
The dollar posted its steepest one-day drop in a month.

14
00:01:15,858 --> 00:01:19,253
Spot gold reclaimed 4,000 dollars.

15
00:01:19,253 --> 00:01:24,939
West Texas crude crashed more than eight percent, breaking below 90 dollars a barrel.

16
00:01:24,939 --> 00:01:31,704
President Trump signaled large-scale strikes on Iran may no longer be needed, but kept military options open.

17
00:01:31,704 --> 00:01:39,020
Cheaper oil eases inflation, lifting industrials, financials, consumer names, and defensives.

18
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:45,282
On the Fed, October ADP payrolls beat expectations with 86,000 new jobs.

19
00:01:45,282 --> 00:01:51,103
Strong hiring gives the Fed room to tighten, and some desks now flag a December hike.

20
00:01:51,103 --> 00:01:57,010
A Democratic Congress also shields Fed independence, limiting White House pressure for cuts.

21
00:01:57,010 --> 00:02:00,920
Markets are no longer pricing a one-way easing cycle.

22
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:03,150
The bull camp is loud.

23
00:02:03,150 --> 00:02:07,599
Goldman Sachs and Deutsche Bank argue this rally has staying power.

24
00:02:07,599 --> 00:02:13,554
Gridlock means fewer policy shocks, and Trump-era corporate tax cuts likely survive.

25
00:02:13,554 --> 00:02:16,312
History also favors bulls.

26
00:02:16,312 --> 00:02:20,184
Stocks usually rise in the twelve months after a midterm.

27
00:02:20,184 --> 00:02:22,733
The skeptics push back hard.

28
00:02:22,733 --> 00:02:27,684
Barclays says the Nasdaq's near ten percent pullback was never about the election.

29
00:02:27,684 --> 00:02:32,402
The real problems are AI revenue delivery and expensive financing.

30
00:02:32,402 --> 00:02:34,719
Neither went away yesterday.

31
00:02:34,719 --> 00:02:38,027
Much of this year's gain is already priced in.

32
00:02:38,027 --> 00:02:42,819
And the debt ceiling looms, likely hitting in the second quarter of next year.

33
00:02:42,819 --> 00:02:47,378
Republicans have roughly eight weeks of unified control left to act.

34
00:02:47,378 --> 00:02:50,442
After that, brinkmanship returns.

35
00:02:50,442 --> 00:02:55,981
Yesterday's pop came largely from hedges being unwound, not from fresh conviction.

36
00:02:55,981 --> 00:02:59,743
Beneath the surface, tech split cleanly in two.

37
00:02:59,743 --> 00:03:05,589
The trigger was Texas, home to 27 percent of U.S. data center power capacity.

38
00:03:05,589 --> 00:03:11,949
Its new Democratic governor campaigned on pausing new data center approvals and scrapping tax breaks.

39
00:03:11,949 --> 00:03:17,219
Nearly two in three Americans oppose AI data centers in their own communities.

40
00:03:17,219 --> 00:03:23,003
Anger over noise, water use, and power bills helped flip deep-red districts.

41
00:03:23,003 --> 00:03:28,800
For the cloud giants, a slower buildout means lighter capex and better free cash flow.

42
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:34,498
Microsoft jumped 6.7 percent to a record high, breaking through a triple top.

43
00:03:34,498 --> 00:03:39,645
It is the only major cloud giant still generating positive free cash flow.

44
00:03:39,645 --> 00:03:42,562
Amazon gained over six percent.

45
00:03:42,562 --> 00:03:45,773
Alphabet added 3.2 percent.

46
00:03:45,773 --> 00:03:50,883
Apple surged on bets that slower data center growth will cap memory prices.

47
00:03:50,883 --> 00:03:54,902
For chip suppliers, the same math runs in reverse.

48
00:03:54,902 --> 00:03:58,248
Their revenue is the cloud giants' capex.

49
00:03:58,248 --> 00:04:04,964
The Philadelphia Semiconductor Index fell 3.1 percent, to 10,268.

50
00:04:04,964 --> 00:04:10,331
That puts 10,000 in focus, a level with several layers of technical support.

51
00:04:10,331 --> 00:04:15,981
If it holds, this correction is likely over, though a V-shaped rebound is unlikely.

52
00:04:15,981 --> 00:04:20,589
If it breaks, the semiconductor bull trend itself may be reversing.

53
00:04:20,589 --> 00:04:24,106
Nvidia has now fallen five straight weeks.

54
00:04:24,106 --> 00:04:27,084
Broadcom and Micron also slid.

55
00:04:27,084 --> 00:04:32,476
Micron's support sits near 740 dollars, then 660.

56
00:04:32,476 --> 00:04:36,630
Chip suppliers in Japan and Korea sold off in sympathy.

57
00:04:36,630 --> 00:04:39,890
The neocloud group took the heaviest damage.

58
00:04:39,890 --> 00:04:42,917
CoreWeave fell 9.9 percent.

59
00:04:42,917 --> 00:04:49,130
Its 2031 unsecured bonds traded as low as 79.5 cents on the dollar.

60
00:04:49,130 --> 00:04:55,822
Its 2030 and 2032 notes now yield above 15 percent, deep in distress territory.

61
00:04:55,822 --> 00:04:58,959
Cipher Mining plunged 20 percent.

62
00:04:58,959 --> 00:05:01,802
IREN dropped over 11 percent.

63
00:05:01,802 --> 00:05:05,086
Nebius lost 6.7 percent.

64
00:05:05,192 --> 00:05:08,254
The buildout supply chain cracked too.

65
00:05:08,204 --> 00:05:11,905
Comfort Systems USA sank 15 percent.

66
00:05:11,905 --> 00:05:14,785
Caterpillar fell over five percent.

67
00:05:14,785 --> 00:05:21,758
Constellation Energy and Vistra, both heavy in Texas, slid on fears of tighter grid and pricing rules.

68
00:05:21,758 --> 00:05:27,763
The medium-term nuclear case still holds, because both parties support a nuclear revival.

69
00:05:27,763 --> 00:05:32,849
Lockheed Martin fell nearly seven percent on fears of defense budget cuts.

70
00:05:32,849 --> 00:05:40,312
Tesla dropped 3.3 percent and SpaceX 3.9 percent, on political pressure, not fundamentals.

71
00:05:40,312 --> 00:05:42,947
Qualcomm reported after the bell.

72
00:05:42,947 --> 00:05:47,542
Revenue of 10.32 billion edged past estimates.

73
00:05:47,542 --> 00:05:53,229
But handset revenue plunged 26 percent, to 5.1 billion dollars.

74
00:05:53,229 --> 00:05:55,753
Shares sank after hours.

75
00:05:55,753 --> 00:05:59,295
Money rotating out of chips and memory is healthy.

76
00:05:59,295 --> 00:06:03,388
Compute and memory remain the long-term backbone of AI.

77
00:06:03,388 --> 00:06:06,170
Here are today's actionable signals.

78
00:06:06,170 --> 00:06:08,952
Nvidia is a buy on weakness.

79
00:06:08,952 --> 00:06:15,337
The cloud giants now hold the pricing power, and working through excess GPU inventory gets harder.

80
00:06:15,337 --> 00:06:19,393
But five weekly losses have flushed out much of the leverage.

81
00:06:19,393 --> 00:06:23,143
Start buying below 199 dollars.

82
00:06:23,143 --> 00:06:27,616
Add at 190, and again at 185.

83
00:06:27,616 --> 00:06:33,131
The risk line is a decisive close below 10,000 on the semiconductor index.

84
00:06:33,131 --> 00:06:35,949
If that breaks, step aside.

85
00:06:35,949 --> 00:06:38,425
Broadcom is a buy at the gap.

86
00:06:38,425 --> 00:06:43,731
Target entries near 315 dollars, the lower edge of the open gap.

87
00:06:43,731 --> 00:06:46,366
The same risk line applies.

88
00:06:46,366 --> 00:06:49,087
Semis must hold 10,000.

89
00:06:49,087 --> 00:06:51,819
Amazon is a sell into strength.

90
00:06:51,819 --> 00:06:58,768
The rally rests on a stretched chain: no war, no tariffs, cheaper goods, stronger e-commerce.

91
00:06:58,768 --> 00:07:03,829
Meanwhile, free cash flow sits tens of billions of dollars in the red.

92
00:07:03,829 --> 00:07:07,273
Take profits on yesterday's six percent pop.

93
00:07:07,273 --> 00:07:11,072
Expect a pullback toward the six-month moving average.

94
00:07:11,072 --> 00:07:13,192
Alphabet is a trim.

95
00:07:13,192 --> 00:07:19,981
Shares rose 3.2 percent and reclaimed the five-day line, but stalled below 300 dollars.

96
00:07:19,981 --> 00:07:24,430
Recent earnings showed AI is starting to hurt search advertising.

97
00:07:24,430 --> 00:07:27,224
That weakens the long-term case.

98
00:07:27,224 --> 00:07:31,109
Sell roughly two-thirds into strength below 300.

99
00:07:31,109 --> 00:07:36,746
Keep a core position, since megacaps favored by Democrats may run a few more days.

100
00:07:36,746 --> 00:07:40,729
On the calendar, IREN reports after today's close.

101
00:07:40,729 --> 00:07:43,180
Treat that one as a coin flip.

102
00:07:43,180 --> 00:07:46,452
CoreWeave reports November 10 after the bell.

103
00:07:46,452 --> 00:07:50,545
Watch its debt, cash flow, and compute delivery.

104
00:07:50,545 --> 00:07:57,297
Nebius follows, with a deeper accumulation zone at 120 to 135 dollars.

105
00:07:57,297 --> 00:08:04,013
Beyond that, watch for an Anthropic IPO pulling cash out of big tech, and the December Fed decision.

106
00:08:04,013 --> 00:08:14,405
Key levels are S&P 7,600 to 7,800, Nasdaq 100 at 28,900, and the semiconductor index at 10,000.

107
00:08:14,405 --> 00:08:18,413
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

108
00:08:18,413 --> 00:08:22,579
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.
